+# Final review: cluster 5 (daemon), epic hg-k4l
+
+Source: base 0dbe4772f4cd541e6b5ecaa1dd55b9a72f62c784, head efc21b65eb225578d3a623b29f23b3688849ea8b (both verified with `git rev-parse --verify <sha>^{commit}`). Caller checkout observed at HEAD efc21b65 with a clean status before and during the review; nothing was edited.
+
+Files: src/daemon/client.rs, src/daemon/doctor.rs, src/daemon/registry.rs (slices 000038-000040).
+
+## Scope and method
+
+Static review of the three slices against the design doc (docs/superpowers/runs/2026-11-05-seat-failure-manager-seats/2026-11-05-seat-failure-manager-seats-design.md, §4.1, §4.2, §4.4 and the Key abstractions section) and its Requirement baseline. Source outside the cluster was opened only to trace the named risks: callers of the new `ClientError::NotInHerdr` variant, callers of `CommandError::from_store`, and the exit-code constant namespace. Tests were not re-run here; the per-task reports record passing scoped runs.
+
+## What the cluster delivers
+
+- `EXIT_DAEMON_UNAVAILABLE = 5` and `EXIT_INTEGRITY = 6` sit next to `EXIT_NO_INSTANCE` and `EXIT_STILL_RUNNING` in src/daemon/client.rs, as §4.4 and the cli::daemon_error contract require. No other exit constant uses 5 or 6 (`EXIT_ALREADY_APPLIED = 4` in src/cli/plan.rs is the only other one).
+- `ClientError::NotInHerdr` replaces `Unavailable("not inside Herdr")` for the Ensure-mode early return, so the classifier can distinguish the exit-2 "not inside a Herdr pane" row from the exit-5 row by type instead of by string. `cli::daemon_error::classify` handles the variant (exit 2), and `seat_failure` maps it to the plain line.
+- `CommandError::integrity` and `CommandError::from_store` give the daemon one store-error classifier (`StoreError::Integrity` to `IpcErrorCode::Integrity`, every other variant to `Internal`), matching §4.2 and the CommandError/IpcErrorCode abstraction. Its production consumers at head are `bootstrap::resolve_error` (seat.resolve, which adds the HEAD revision and node-manager suffix on integrity and delegates every other variant to `from_store`) and the daemon `who` handler in src/threads/mod.rs.
+- Tests: a socket-pair test that a `Remote{Integrity}` frame decodes to `ClientError::Remote { code: Integrity }`; constructor and classifier tests covering `Integrity` and all six other `StoreError` variants.
+- doctor.rs only adds `default_manager: None` to a test fixture for the new teamspace field.
+
+## Tracing the named risks
+
+- NotInHerdr callers: every Ensure-mode caller (ops, plan, request, seat, status writer.resume, undo) now passes client errors to `daemon_error::report` or `seat_failure`, so no site that used to catch `Unavailable(_)` silently stops catching the not-in-Herdr case. `status` (NoEnsure) still falls back to `direct_status` on `Unavailable`, and NoEnsure never yields `NotInHerdr`, so that fallback keeps working. The task-2 deferred note about the lost "daemon unavailable:" prefix for anyhow-printing Ensure callers no longer applies at head, because no Ensure caller prints via anyhow any more.
+- Ensure mode with `HERDR_SOCKET_PATH` unset returns `NotInHerdr` before connecting, so a running daemon's `Remote` reply never reaches such a caller. Base already returned early here, and for a mutating or seat command outside a pane, exit 2 is what the spec's not-in-Herdr row asks for.
+- Hello socket mismatch and request-id mismatch still come back as `Unavailable`, and `Remote{Unavailable}` / `Remote{VersionMismatch}` from `hello` pass through `?`. All of these map to exit 5 in the classifier, which covers the "wrong Herdr", "still starting" and "version mismatch" rows of §4.4.
+- Other daemon handlers still map store errors with `CommandError::internal(e.to_string())`: `store.head()` in who, bootstrap's `internal` helper, `PlanError::Store` in src/plan/commands.rs, and undo. Per §4.2 and the Key abstractions section, the classifier is required for seat_reply, who and show, and those are covered. Whether plan or undo should also surface integrity as exit 6 is a cross-cluster question for the seam reviewer, not a defect in this cluster.
+
+## Requirement baseline rows touching this cluster
+
+- B2 / B3 (daemon down: tell the agent to retry, carry on): this cluster supplies the exit-5 constant and the typed `NotInHerdr`/`Unavailable` split. The path from the CLI entry points to the stderr text runs through cli::daemon_error and cli::seat, which belong to the cli cluster and the seam reviewer.
+- B4 (named non-transient integrity error): this cluster supplies `IpcErrorCode::Integrity` at the CommandError level, the one classifier, and the exit-6 constant. It is wired into the production `seat.resolve` and `who` handlers. The end-to-end path (unresolved reference, then `StoreError::Integrity`, then `resolve_error`, then IPC, then exit 6) crosses the bootstrap, ipc-rs and cli clusters; that is the seam reviewer's to trace.
+- No baseline row is owned only by this cluster, and nothing this cluster contributes is missing.
+
+## Findings
+
+- src/daemon/client.rs `call_daemon` doc comment says "on failure with CallMode::Ensure: if HERDR_SOCKET_PATH is unset → NotInHerdr". The check actually runs before any connect attempt, not after a failure. The base comment had the same wording. Cosmetic only.
+
+## Verdict
+ready
+
+## Must fix before landing
+- none
+
+## Untested scope
+- No test drives `call_daemon_with_timeout` in Ensure mode with HERDR_SOCKET_PATH unset to assert it returns `ClientError::NotInHerdr`; only the classifier and seat_failure unit tests use the variant, built by hand (spawned-binary coverage may exist in tests/seat_exit_codes.rs, which belongs to the tests cluster).
+- Tests for this cluster were not re-run in this review; it relies on the passing scoped runs recorded in the task reports.
+
+## Deferred OK
+- `call_daemon` doc comment wording ("on failure") is inaccurate about when the NotInHerdr check runs; the base had the same wording.
+- Daemon handlers other than seat.resolve and who (plan `PlanError::Store`, undo, `store.head()` failures) still map store errors to Internal rather than through `from_store`; the spec does not require them to, and the seam reviewer owns cross-cluster integrity surfacing.
